d as well  in terms of how the data is being  reported to us  we will be getting access to the data  via an  api um we are very excited  so the city council will have access to  um person-level data um  so there's privacy concerns here um  so we only have access to this data set  for a  period of time every year  and the public so advocates really  wanted  this data to be available for the public  to view  and analyze but due to privacy issues  it was agreed upon to make public an  aggregated version  and not all the variables um  so this is already out there if anyone's  interested  i guess if we share slides or you can  search on the open data portal  for the local law it's out there but um  we're very we just so this is the  timeline um before covid of when we  would  receive access um to the api  so we just got access to it a week ago  and we're in the middle  of just digging into the data we have no  immediate findings yet we're we we know  that we have to deal with missing data  we  don't know how consistent employee  information is reported across all the  agencies  but that's something that we're very  excited to  dig into and maybe at a future point or  i'm sure there will probably be a  release of the findings and  we'll um we'll be in the news  um okay so moving on to our last  data function um it's data visualization  so communicating our findings in a way  that is easily understandable by  everyone and pushing  our work onto github or the council web  page  for others to add and collaborate on is  uh  the main goal of this um process  so an example of a project  um that does this is  evictions in new york city um so this  project we worked on it  when the speaker of the council was  held another position at the same time  the interim public advocate  the former public advocate went on to  another role  so i guess due to law he becomes the  interim public advocate  so evictions is a big issue area for the  council  the council passed also another  legislation first of its kind called  uh right to council so this legislation  seeks to  decrease or address the eviction crisis  in new york city by  providing um free legal assistance  to those going through an evictions  proceeding um it's being rolled out  in stages so at one point um  i have 20 something i'm not too sure  it'll be available across the whole city  um but also this remains an ongoing  issue  um due to covid there was there's a  moratorium on evictions but  at the end of the year it will be lifted  unless it's extended  so there might be a big rise in  evictions um  if it's not extended um so we  put this page together to showcase um  the council's bill and to sort of to see  um the facts of this bill  there has been decreases in evictions in  the city  and also just to show where it still  remains uh  an issue as you can see in the bronx  there's a lot of  evictions there um so in this process  of putting the charts and the maps  together  the data source the victim data set on  the open data portal  lacked latitude and longitude columns  despite having address information so  the addresses  were very dirty so if you were to put  them  in a geocoder it wouldn't um spit out  the latitude and longitude for you it  would give you an n  a so what we did was did a script that  cleaned the addresses  um so using g sub and regular  expressions to clean the addresses  we were able to get the latitude and  longitudes  using the new york city's geocoding api  and that led us to be able to map at  this fine level  before that the only maps of evictions  in the city were at the zip code level  so advocates were really excited to see  these maps out there and um they  the housing data advocates reached out  to us thanking for  our work and putting the code out there  it helped them um  cross check their own work that they've  been doing on evictions and  hopefully expand on our scope of the  project at that time  so this sort of hits all our points and  goals of  data visualization this project  [Music]  so i guess to start wrapping up um  maybe brooke do you want to tell us what  our  has been our success strategies and um  embedding data at the council sure um  so i guess the main reason the reason  why we exist is because we have support  from  leadership and higher ups and that our  speaker of the city council  does really believe in data and using  data to inform policy and legislation  and  all of our bosses really do believe in  that too  and it's um and lawyers who are writing  the laws are really receptive to like  conversations with us about  how to use data and how to  think about data in like new and  different ways when it comes to  legislation  um but i think like and these like sort  of strengths predate me because i'm  newer than rose and much of the team but  um  that we have a strong like sort of core  like visualization effort and this um  this sort of like goal or this um  ethos of like  making our findings really interpretable  and legible to the general public we  strongly believe that everyone should be  able to understand our results  and that's um really big especially when  you're dealing with so many different  audiences like non-technical and  technical  um like everyone should be able to  understand things and  visualization i think is really  important for that  um and then we have some really strong  um  quantitative and technical people on our  team that can  like in creative ways reframe these  you know legislative issues or these  constituent issues almost  into quantitative scientific um  questions and so we're i think we're  really good at like  incorporating the scientific method and  just using our  scientific backgrounds and making that  really like  integral into the legislative process  um and that's yeah those are things i'm  really excited about  rose do you want to talk about the  things that are hard  there are challenges and that we're  slowly chipping at  and still maybe walls to  climb over um so sometimes  due to timelines or the staff are just  too busy  or maybe even they forget us they bring  us in too late  so there's a question a problem that  they want us  to help with and they're like can you do  this by tomorrow  and we're like uh well probably not  so we don't have the opportunity at that  time to go  in depth or like in depth enough to give  them  an adequate correct response to their  question and that also feeds into  managing expectations  sometimes they think we can do magic but  sometimes we're limited to the quality  of the data the data quality is poor  we can't give um sort of estimates with  a certain amount of certainty  um things like that and then lastly our  own projects  uh most of the time our work is very  reactive to what's  in the news or agendas  of the the council and other council  members  so long-term projects that we have  our time on them is limited  compared to the amount of time we spend  on um  these more reactive short-term projects  um so i guess to summarize um  our role at the council is new  and um i guess for the better for  new yorkers and the laws that are coming  out of it before  there was no scientific or  quantitative thinking put into a lot of  these laws  so sometimes like um  so like more thought is going to towards  um  like numbers that are being um written  into the law  so or um processes have  gone through like some sort of check of  what's happening  what's the data telling us and maybe  that sometimes this  can contradict what a constituent thinks  is happening on their block and they  assume that that's happening everywhere  in new york city  um so i think we've in a short amount of  time we've  done a lot but we still have some  challenges um  i don't know brooke if you want to add  anything i mean we're a team that has  grown from i think it was just  rose and one other maybe two other  people  and um like the confidence of the growth  like the confidence in what we do is  like um  sort of represented by how much our team  has grown  and how um yeah like  the like work that we've sort of done  at a micro and macro scale whether it be  like here's the data set that you need  and here's that like  mean that you are looking for to like  more sophisticated models to understand  like really complex problems so i just  think yeah  we have a and we work on a lot of  different projects  a lot of different topics so you're not  working on one  thing forever you're working on housing  and then you're working on  greenhouse gases and then you're working  on you know  i don't know a million other things  so it's so thanks to everyone that's  watching  um here is our contact info for our team  um our github page um  our website and our twitter handles so  if you guys have any questions feel free  to reach out to us  and thank you again for watching  we still have more ahead for you  and i'll show you that in a second but i  do think that when they talk about how  to make a bill  did anybody else think that they should  make a city version of schoolhouse rock  i think that would be great to see a  thing involving schoolhouse rock  i think that would be pretty awesome
 

